      "worked": "The absolute value equals $1$ when $2-\\sqrt{a-x^2}=1$ or $2-\\sqrt{a-x^2}=-1$. Therefore $\\sqrt{a-x^2}=1$ or $\\sqrt{a-x^2}=3$.\n\nThese give $x^2=a-1$ or $x^2=a-9$, respectively. Each positive right-hand side gives two solutions, a zero right-hand side gives only $x=0$, and a negative right-hand side gives none.\n\nFor $a<1$ there are no solutions; for $a=1$ there is one. For $1<a<9$, only $x^2=a-1$ contributes, giving exactly $x=\\pm\\sqrt{a-1}$. At $a=9$ there are three solutions, $x=0,\\pm\\sqrt8$. For $a>9$, both equations contribute two distinct solutions, giving four in total.\n\nHence exactly two distinct solutions occur when\n$$1<a<9.$$",
